  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "note-jeppson-48",
      "type": "text_bubble",
      "position": {
        "x": -74,
        "y": 1352
      },
      "width": 460,
      "height": 560,
      "style": {
        "width": 460,
        "height": 560
      },
      "data": {
        "label": "Note",
        "title": "JEPPSON (1974) 48-PIPE NETWORK",
        "text": "Validation case: the 48-pipe network of R. W. Jeppson, Steady Flow Analysis of Pipe Networks: An Instructional Manual, Utah State University, 1974, Ch. VI, example problem 3 (network p. 71, solution p. 70).\n\nData: all pipes Darcy-Weisbach with roughness 0.012 in; water at 68 °F (not stated for this problem); all junctions at one elevation. Pipe labels give the published diameter and length. Supplies 20.428 cfs (flow source) and 56.312 cfs (tank); 28 demands of 1.0 to 5.0 cfs and 1.74 cfs (flow sources with negative set flow).\n\nJeppson gives no heads for this network: the tank level is an arbitrary head reference, and only flows and friction losses compare. With one pressure boundary the flows depend on the pipes alone.\n\nEach junction joining more than three connections is a chain of tees with short links, so the diagram is busier than the source figure. Pipes are drawn in the direction of the published flow, except P16, P22, P33, drawn against it so every tee chain has an outlet."
      }
    },
